        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>De Incarnatione Verbi §25</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;He that committeth sin is of the devil; for the devil sinneth from the beginning. For this purpose the Son of God was manifested, that he might destroy the works of the devil&#x27; — Athanasius marks eis touto ephanerōthē ho Huios tou theou hina lysē ta erga tou diabolou (for THIS purpose was-manifested the Son-of-God, that he might LOOSEN-DOWN/DESTROY the works of the devil) as the SOTERIOLOGICAL-PURPOSE-STATEMENT of the Incarnation. The ephanerōthē (was-manifested) is the technical-incarnational verb — the eternal-Word-who-IS becomes-visibly-manifest in flesh-and-action. The hina-purpose clause names the polemical-redemptive-telos: not abstractly &#x27;salvation&#x27; but specifically destroying-the-works-of-the-devil — sin, death, deception, the cosmic-tyrant. Pairs with Heb 2:14 (hina dia tou thanatou katargēsē ton to kratos echonta tou thanatou, tout&#x27; estin ton diabolon — that through-death he-might-destroy the one having the power-of-death, that is, the devil) as the canonical-incarnational-destroying-the-devil pair.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, De Incarnatione Verbi (On the Incarnation), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 25 (1857).",
